{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 370,
    "dDurationMs": 3170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK kids, let’s talk about states of matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3540,
    "dDurationMs": 1730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You know your states of matter don’t you?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5270,
    "dDurationMs": 8010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have solids, liquids and gasses, and plasmas, quark-gluon plasmas, nuclear matter, bose-einstein"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13280,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "condensates, neutronium, time crystals, and sand."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17780,
    "dDurationMs": 3490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Come to think of it, maybe I don’t know\nmy states of matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21270,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or what a state of matter even is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23510,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see if we can figure it out together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31705,
    "dDurationMs": 5744,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You know what a state of matter is. There's solids, there's liquids, there's gases - different states that the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37449,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "element or molecule could occupy based on the strength of their chemical bonds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42239,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The strong bonds in a solid keep the material rigid, but heat it up and those bonds break"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47519,
    "dDurationMs": 4081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we’re left with weaker bonds that allow the particles to slip and slide around each"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51600,
    "dDurationMs": 5059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other while nonetheless generally sticking together, leaving us with a liquid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56659,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Heat it further and the weak bonds break,\nallowing particles to fly freely around the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60989,
    "dDurationMs": 3181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "room - and voila, a gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64170,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of you may have also learned what happens if we keep heating things up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69190,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electrons are knocked free from atoms, breaking all molecular bonds in the process and creating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74250,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a Plasma."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75250,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So cool, states of matter are just the different, well, states that atoms can be in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81500,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But wait, the quarks inside protons and neutrons are “matter”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85280,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What is the state of matter of those?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88030,
    "dDurationMs": 3630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Does it depend on the state of matter of the atoms they’re part of?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91660,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And what about materials like sand or glass, which may have different properties to their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96380,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "component parts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98140,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then there are all those pop-sci media\nclaims of “new state of matter discovered”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103110,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- time crystals being a recent example."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104950,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Are they for real?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107220,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To answer these questions, we better figure out what a state of matter really is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111500,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you were to base your reasoning on the states of matter you learned in school - solid,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115610,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "liquid, and gas, and plasma for those who\nstayed in school too long - a simple pattern"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120580,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is apparent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122110,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Change in temperature results in change in state, or in a phase."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126750,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Phase shifts occur at temperatures specific to the material, for example ice melts into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132780,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water when temperature rises above the 273 Kelvin mark; then evaporates into a gas a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138390,
    "dDurationMs": 4739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hundred Kelvin higher, and ionizes into a\nplasma at several thousand Kelvin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143129,
    "dDurationMs": 3591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course it’s a bit more complicated than this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146720,
    "dDurationMs": 5549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Transition temperatures depend on the material, but they also depend on pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152269,
    "dDurationMs": 4041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, water boils and freezes at a\nlower temperature on a mountain top where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156310,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the air pressure is low."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158810,
    "dDurationMs": 6019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead of a 1-dimensional relationship between phase and temperature, think of a 2-dimensional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164829,
    "dDurationMs": 3611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relationship with both temperature and pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168440,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call this a phase diagram."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171500,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It shows us that things are much more complicated than solid, liquid, gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175700,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, there are secret hidden states of matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179640,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, at temperatures and pressures above the critical point, the line between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184560,
    "dDurationMs": 7530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas and liquid blurs and we have a supercritical fluid which shares properties of both."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192090,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The two numbers related on the phase diagram - temperature and pressure - are statistical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197810,
    "dDurationMs": 2910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties of a large collection of particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200720,
    "dDurationMs": 5129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A single water molecule doesn’t really have a temperature, it has a velocity - but the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205849,
    "dDurationMs": 4051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "average energy of motion of all water molecules is its temperature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209900,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A state of matter defines how these various average properties relate to each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214650,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It defines what we call the equation of state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217840,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example in an ideal gas, pressure is proportional to temperature and inversely proportional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223090,
    "dDurationMs": 1799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224889,
    "dDurationMs": 2690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Different states of matter have different\nequations of state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227579,
    "dDurationMs": 4341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general the field of physics that studies\nthe relationships between the statistical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231920,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties of different states of matter is\ncalled thermodynamics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237030,
    "dDurationMs": 6359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But a state of matter determines and is determined by much more than the thermodynamic properties, subjective"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243389,
    "dDurationMs": 3781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "qualities, like, say, “wetness\" of water\n- is an emergent property of that state of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247170,
    "dDurationMs": 4219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter; one not possessed by the component H20 molecules."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251389,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it’s typically the non-thermodynamic\nproperties that formally distinguish one state"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255609,
    "dDurationMs": 1141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from another."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256750,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example solids are rigid having effectively infinite viscosity; liquids are viscous and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262530,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are incompressible; gasses are compressible and diffuse evenly to fill any size container."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268330,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general, we call some collection of stuff a state of matter if it has sufficiently unique set of emergent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274580,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behaviors - like the extremely low electrical resistance of a superconductor, or the near"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280210,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "absence of viscosity in a superfluid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283170,
    "dDurationMs": 5249,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so a state of matter is an emergent behavior due to the interactions between components"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288419,
    "dDurationMs": 1961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under particular conditions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290380,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Does that mean we can make different states of matter from things other than atoms?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294580,
    "dDurationMs": 1089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It does."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295669,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start our search for some new states\nof matter by exploring in the direction we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300889,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "started."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301889,
    "dDurationMs": 4051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What happens if we increase the temperature of a plasma?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305940,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A plasma still consists of composite particles: the electrons are elementary, but the atomic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312090,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuclei are little bundles of nucleons - protons and neutrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even in a hydrogen plasma, the lone protons are bundles of quarks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321800,
    "dDurationMs": 4679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just as we tore apart the atom when we made our plasma, if we crank temperature up high enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326479,
    "dDurationMs": 2451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can destroy nucleons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328930,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although we’re going to need it fairly hot\n- around 7 trillion Kelvin, due to the extremely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333680,
    "dDurationMs": 2959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "high binding energies of nucleons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336639,
    "dDurationMs": 5521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the Hagedorn temperature, and when we reach it quarks are stripped from nucleons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342160,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to produce a quark-gluon plasma."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344970,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is our next state of matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347120,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might wonder if this stuff is even more plasma-like that plasma - with the particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351140,
    "dDurationMs": 2540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more free to zip around the room."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353680,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But actually, the interactions between the\ngluons and quarks remain significant and so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358680,
    "dDurationMs": 3590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a quark-gluon plasma behaves more like a liquid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362270,
    "dDurationMs": 5209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We routinely make this stuff in our particle\naccelerators, but the quantity is tiny - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367479,
    "dDurationMs": 2951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "result of smashing two nucleons together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370430,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However in the very early universe everything was a quark-gluon plasma, and that may also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375740,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be true in the cores of massive neutron stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378710,
    "dDurationMs": 6970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if a quark-gluon plasma is liquid-like, does that mean it can freeze?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can, and its frozen form is a nucleon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390720,
    "dDurationMs": 6740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More general, a hadron, so protons and neutrons but also various exotic combinations of quarks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397460,
    "dDurationMs": 6799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A hadron is fairly literally a crystal of\nquark-gluon plasma - it’s the stuff in its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404259,
    "dDurationMs": 1261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“solid” form."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405520,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s right, you are made of quark snow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408680,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact the whole process of creating quark-gluon plasma is like smashing snowballs in the middle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414340,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the arctic, hoping to produce a few droplets of water, which freeze again almost instantly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420430,
    "dDurationMs": 6209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The stuff of quarks is generically called\nquark matter or QCD matter - for quantum chromodynamics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426639,
    "dDurationMs": 2611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- the physics of quark and gluon interactions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429250,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To fully convince you that quark matter has its own states of matter, behold its phase"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435220,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diagram."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436240,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here it's temperature versus baryonic potential instead of pressure, which is basically how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441550,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much energy quarks can absorb or emit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444130,
    "dDurationMs": 6591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our quark-gluon plasma is actually the analogy of gas in atomic matter, even if it’s behavior"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450721,
    "dDurationMs": 1109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is more liquid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451830,
    "dDurationMs": 2489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our hadrons are our “solids”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454319,
    "dDurationMs": 5501,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you want to see what it’s like to move\nto right on this diagram, just burrow into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459820,
    "dDurationMs": 1730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a neutron star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461550,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First the individual quark “crystals”\nmerge together into a fluid of neutrons that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467080,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we call neutronium, and then the neutrons\ndissolve and we end up with really bizarre"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471590,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forms of liquid-like quark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474340,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The states of matter we’re most familiar\nwith can be explained as particles interacting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478530,
    "dDurationMs": 2050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under classical forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480580,
    "dDurationMs": 4899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But once you bring quantum mechanics into the picture, many strange states of matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485479,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "become possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487139,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, in degenerate matter like neutronium or Bose-Einstein condensates, all quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493379,
    "dDurationMs": 6931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states are occupied, leading to some surprising and useful emergent properties, like superconductivity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500310,
    "dDurationMs": 1629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and superfluidity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501939,
    "dDurationMs": 4581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Time crystals are the latest and perhaps weirdest quantum state of matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506520,
    "dDurationMs": 4311,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are configurations of entangled particles that oscillate between states even when they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510831,
    "dDurationMs": 1439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have no energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512270,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In regular thermodynamics, the lowest energy corresponds to absolute zero temperature,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517110,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which in turn means zero motion of particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520440,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The lowest energy state of a time crystal\ninvolves real motion, which makes them thermodynamically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525990,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different than other states of matter - so\nthey qualify as a state of matter of their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530410,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531410,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it sounds like states of matter really\nare … exactly that - states of matter, rather"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536370,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than states of atoms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538950,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Subatomic particles can have their own states of matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541810,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it turns out that two completely different states of matter can exist simultaneously"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545850,
    "dDurationMs": 1510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at different scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547360,
    "dDurationMs": 7430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, liquid water contains many little nuggets of frozen “solid” nuclear material."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554790,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Different states of matter can be sort of nested within each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559570,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that’s true for the subatomic states\nwithin the atomic states, what about states"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564250,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formed by components larger than atoms and molecules?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567850,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example sand?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570510,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each grain of sand is a solid, but when you\nmake air flow through sand you can change"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576870,
    "dDurationMs": 5149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how those grains of sand interact with each other, and the sand will start acting like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582019,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a liquid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583019,
    "dDurationMs": 3801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When this happens, objects that are light\nenough will float from the bottom to the surface,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586820,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something that would never ever happen with\na solid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589800,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet, the grains of sand never stopped\nbeing solid and the air never stopped being"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593490,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594490,
    "dDurationMs": 6709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s something we don’t usually think\nof as particles: human beings, but they can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601199,
    "dDurationMs": 3791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behave in ways eerily close to states of matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604990,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a fairly diffuse crowd, people will walk\naround steering to miss each other with no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610540,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trouble."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611540,
    "dDurationMs": 2630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The crowd behaves in some ways like a gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614170,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you increase the people-density to\naround 5 per square meter or more, a phase"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619820,
    "dDurationMs": 1250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transition occurs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621070,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It starts behaving like a liquid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624250,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The frequent interactions between people cause liquid-like phenomena like currents and waves"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629480,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as individuals lose their autonomy of motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632740,
    "dDurationMs": 3789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is known as a crowd crush, and it can be very dangerous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636529,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately, physicists can come to the rescue here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640279,
    "dDurationMs": 4061,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Using what we know about gases and liquids - about thermodynamics - it’s possible to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644340,
    "dDurationMs": 5059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spot an impending crowd crush and avoid it by changing the “thermodynamic properties”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649399,
    "dDurationMs": 1430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the crowd."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650829,
    "dDurationMs": 5051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Namely, lower the density when you see it’s approaching a phase transition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655880,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it doesn’t stop with people."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657660,
    "dDurationMs": 5619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Astrophysicists routinely model the galaxies as a sort of fluid of stars, where the interactions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663279,
    "dDurationMs": 2391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are not electromagnetic, but gravitational."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665670,
    "dDurationMs": 7460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, galaxies are fluids of stars which themselves are made of plasmas of hydrogen made of frozen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673130,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuggets of quark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674800,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if sand and crowds and galaxies exhibit behaviors that resemble states of matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680860,
    "dDurationMs": 3349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are these really actual states of matter?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684209,
    "dDurationMs": 4031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not technically, but that’s really just\na matter of convention."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688240,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fact is, the concept of \"states of matter\" can help us to understand many kinds of interactions,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694750,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even between macroscopic “particles”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697810,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Max Tegmark from MIT has proposed that consciousness itself can be understood as a state of matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705610,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like the characteristic properties of\na regular state of matter, the conscious mind"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709980,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is an emergent property of a type of information system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714120,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The analogs of temperature, pressure, etc.\nare informational parameters like memory,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720160,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "computation, and informational integration; the right combination of which lead to specific"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726100,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emergent properties like self awareness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729380,
    "dDurationMs": 6850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thinking of the mind as a state of matter\nallows us to use the tools of our material"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736230,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sciences - for example quantum mechanics and condensed matter physics - to help us understand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741540,
    "dDurationMs": 2739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why we see the world the way we do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744279,
    "dDurationMs": 3752,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or at least, so claims Max Tegmark."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748031,
    "dDurationMs": 5729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The definition of state of matter is somewhat slippery; its clear enough when we talk about the common"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753760,
    "dDurationMs": 5199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states, but gets a bit blurry on the boundaries or in exotic cases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758959,
    "dDurationMs": 4921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Despite this, concept is incredibly useful\nfor helping us understand the behavior of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763880,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physical systems - from the instant after\nthe big bang to the behavior of crowds, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768120,
    "dDurationMs": 2570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps to the nature of the conscious mind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770690,
    "dDurationMs": 5089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just think of our universe as nested layers\nof states of matter, from the smallest to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775779,
    "dDurationMs": 3391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the largest scales of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779170,
    "dDurationMs": 4169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I wanted to let you know about PBS Terra."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783339,
    "dDurationMs": 7071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "PBS Terra is the home of even more great science and nature shows from PBS Digital Studios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790410,
    "dDurationMs": 4229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And they're always asking fascinating questions with rigorous answers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794639,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example if you want to know what would happen if you lived forever?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798259,
    "dDurationMs": 4601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then check out “Far Out” which explores\nthe future of science, technology, and culture,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802860,
    "dDurationMs": 4099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and how these changes may affect humanity and all life on Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806959,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or if you'd rather know why you're still watching this video?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810339,
    "dDurationMs": 5391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Check out “Why Am I Like This” which looks at the evolutionary biology of the human body"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815730,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and how we ended up with all these quirks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818510,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's links in the description for all the\ngreat content on PBS Terra."
    } ]
  } ]
}
